# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **Exactly one read**, answering a shape or an array of them, detached from the database so
# nothing downstream can lazily load or write through what it was handed.
#
# **No Result.** Finding nothing is an answer, not a failure; an envelope would make every
# caller unwrap a value that was never in doubt. A refusal raises instead.
#
# A query never calls a query — two reads wearing one name, and the shape an N+1 arrives in.
class Query
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission
<%- end -%>

  # A query has no Result to carry a refusal, and wrapping one would make every caller
  # unwrap a value that was never in doubt. So it raises, like every other query failure.
  #
  # An operation implementing `anonymous_call` instead of `call` runs before anyone is
  # identified and is not checked. That is a property of the class, never of the caller.
<%- if auth -%>
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    raise Permission::Refused, permission unless permits?(actor)

    perform(actor, arguments)
  end

  # **Setup asks what state is legal, not who may reach it.** A test building a booking to
  # cancel is not testing who may build one, and assembling a grant bag to get there teaches
  # nothing. It skips the permission check and nothing else — a query writes no audit entry to
  # skip. A test uses it for the subject too: authorisation is class-sized, so an operation has
  # no authorisation behaviour of its own to assert.
  #
  # **It raises outside the test environment.** That is why it is a method rather than an actor
  # that says yes to everything: such an actor is a value, and a value can be built anywhere.
  def self.test_call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless defined?(::Rails) && ::Rails.env.test?
      raise "#{name}.test_call is for tests: there is no unchecked door outside the test environment"
    end

    perform(actor, arguments)
  end

  # The half of `call` that runs once the door has been opened. Both entry points share it,
  # so the only difference between them is the check itself.
  def self.perform(actor, arguments)
    operation = new(**arguments)
    answer = operation.__perform__(actor)
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    answer = new(**arguments).__perform__
<%- end -%>

    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with shapes, got #{answer.class}" unless shapes?(answer)

    answer
  end
<%- if auth -%>
  private_class_method :perform
<%- end -%>

  def self.shapes?(answer)
    return answer.all? { |entry| entry.is_a?(Shape) } if answer.is_a?(Array)

    answer.is_a?(Shape)
  end
  private_class_method :shapes?

<%- if auth -%>

  private

  # Whoever the door was opened for.
  attr_reader :actor

<%- end -%>

  # Implicit receiver, so it works whether the entry point is public or private. `send` would
  # too and is banned: a base class that writes it cannot ask an application not to. Public and
  # unreachable all the same, because `new` is private.
<%- if auth -%>
  public def __perform__(actor)
    # **The actor belongs to the base class, not to the operation's signature.** Forcing
    # `actor:` into every initializer would put a keyword in fifty constructors so three could
    # read it. Set here, and available through the private reader below.
    @actor = actor

    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
  end
<%- else -%>
  public def __perform__
    call
  end
<%- end -%>

  # **The only way in is `call`.** `allocate` is listed because it is the hole `new` alone
  # leaves: public on every class, skips `initialize`, and `X.allocate.__perform__(actor)` ran
  # the operation unauthenticated.
  private_class_method :new, :allocate
end
